  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 131 PM EDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nVery warm again this afternoon ahead of the front, and highs are \nflirting with daily records again at CHA, OQT, and TRI. Today's \nrecord highs are below: \n\nDate    Chattanooga    Knoxville      Tri-Cities     Oak Ridge      \n10-16   85(1953)       87(1897)       83(1984)       83(2016)  \n\nA strong cold front will be pushing through our area this afternoon, \nwith gusty winds and some showers.  A few rumble of thunder cannot \nbe ruled out far north, but CAPE looks very limited. We will see \nsome wind gusts approaching or in a few spots possibly exceeding 30 \nmph this afternoon, and these gusty winds will linger through \ntonight over some of the higher elevations. The showers will \ncontinue behind the front tonight as a deep mid/upper level trough \ndigs through the region, then taper off late tonight with just a few \nshowers lingering into Saturday mainly over the higher elevations. \nAs much colder air pushes in behind the front, some snow will likely \nmix in over the higher mountain peaks later tonight into early \nSaturday before the precipitation ends. Snow may even whiten the \nground with very light snow accumulation over the highest peaks late \ntonight/early Saturday before it melts.  \n\nThe weekend will be cool with high temperatures well below seasonal \nnormals Saturday and still several degrees below normal Sunday. We \nwill likely see the first frost of the season for portions of the \narea Saturday night.  Right now the question for frost Saturday \nnight is how much wind will be stirring, which could keep the frost \nconfined more to sheltered valleys.  It will be mostly clear again \nand winds will likely be lighter Sunday night, with frost again \npossible especially north and in outlying areas further south. No \nfrost advisories, since we are past the date for ending \nthe issuance of frost/freeze products for the fall. \n\nHigh pressure is forecast to be in control as we head into next \nweek.  Monday through Friday look dry with a gradual warming trend.",
